{"parentUuid":"dde5011e-0312-434b-aac8-f50f4fad132c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"Baseline tests pass.  I’ve clarified the follow-up test plan: I’ll create a new test file modeled on existing relevant test files, and the test scenario includes specified account configuration, payment split, fee calculation, refund operation and balance verification logic. I’ve finalized the detailed test logic: I’ll add query blocking checks during batch processing, cover two scenarios after gate release (both operations succeed or r2 fails), require query to return correct statuses and balances, and add an extra unrelated account-asset combo to verify sorting rules. I’ve confirmed the specific test data configuration: aa/usdc holds 1000 units and bb/eth holds 7, with balances view sorted in the order of aa/usdc then bb/eth. Refunds only affect aa/usdc, and bb/eth balances remain unchanged across both test scenarios. I’ve also clarified the refund processing rules and the requirement that queries must succeed without ledger_closed or storage_error errors.\n\nI’ve further finalized the test coverage for the follow-up plan: I’ll add checks for re-query returning identical business state and unchanged file bytes across multiple queries, and include extra validations to catch premature return of stale snapshots.\n\nI’ve confirmed the blocking check is critical to avoid incorrect final state matching mid-batch state in failure scenarios. I’ll run tests with the established channel + Gosched pattern from existing interleave tests and the -race flag. The persist gate call counting logic is validated, and the expected snapshot balances for the success scenario are confirmed.\n\nFailure scenario expected balances: aa/usdc = 700, bb/eth = 7. I’ve confirmed settlement and refund parameters, and require query snapshots to return balances sorted as aa/usdc then bb/eth.\n\nI’ve finalized additional test validations: I’ll verify re-query returns identical business state and unchanged file bytes, plus persistence via disk reopen. I’ll also check r2’s batch failure status, error requirements and per-item storage error rule.\n\nI’ve finalized more test requirements: queries must start after the gate is reached, snapshots must be deep-equal to expected states, test files follow the existing naming and directory conventions, and I’ll reuse the second persist gate. I’ll start writing the test file now. I’ve started writing the test file, and have confirmed its package declaration and core test function skeleton.\n\nI’ve completed the test file writing, with two corresponding test functions covering both test scenarios. I’ve finalized all test parameters including expected refund records, settlement records, balance configurations and scenario-specific adjustment rules. The test cases include refund error checks, status and record validation, deep equality checks for snapshots, consistency verification of read results across multiple queries, persistence validation after disk reopen, and confirmation that failed refund operations do not block subsequent valid refunds. I’ll reuse the existing cross-package deep equality assertion utility to ensure test reliability.\n\nI've confirmed the test logic for verifying that failed refund transactions do not occupy successful sequence numbers. The existing sequential test covers this scenario comprehensively, and I'll add a lightweight regression case for the interleave test, with the retry operation placed after read-only and file consistency checks to keep the test logic clean.\n\nI've confirmed the test implementation details: only the failure scenario proof will be added to the interleave test, idempotency checks for success scenarios are excluded as existing tests cover this, and the execution order of test steps has been finalized.\n\nI’ve confirmed the retry failure scenario uses a distinct transaction ID for the second refund to differentiate it from the original failed one, and I’ll keep the current test focused on verifying transaction sequence number logic, with reuse validation covered by other existing tests.\n\nI’ve finalized all remaining test specifications, covering error handling, re-query state consistency, file byte integrity, and validating that only ongoing refunds trigger ledger changes during queries. I’ve confirmed all snapshot and balance rules, and will write the test file now.\n\nI’ll run relevant tests multiple times for stability, execute full test suites and code format checks. I’ll add language-specific header comments following repo style, confirm test cleanup logic is valid, and write the test now.\n","type":"thinking"}],"id":"msg_14e94768e372a5a7f1e3b503","model":"auto_model/urm","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":5586,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":100152,"output_tokens":3690,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"stop_details":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"type":"assistant","uuid":"877bc158-07a7-48d9-9e4e-8e4855e231f1","timestamp":"2026-10-07T06:58:54.624Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"12b52d87-17ec-4fe8-a0a9-321baebe2f15","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/opt/gsb-fleet/workspaces/inst-16/B/task048","sessionId":"12b52d87-17ec-4fe8-a0a9-321baebe2f15","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
{"parentUuid":"877bc158-07a7-48d9-9e4e-8e4855e231f1","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"Baseline is green and I now understand the established interleave-test pattern (`secondPersistGate` + two handles + non-blocking \"must still be waiting\" check, parameterized over both save outcomes). The existing `reconcile_refund_interleave_test.go` proves this for reconcile; there is no equivalent for the public `Ledger.Query`. I'll add that regression test.\n","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_14e94768e372a5a7f1e3b503","model":"auto_model/urm","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":5586,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":100152,"output_tokens":3690,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"stop_details":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"type":"assistant","uuid":"5f39bc13-096f-4ca6-8452-9945f5e4b549","timestamp":"2026-10-07T06:58:56.219Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"12b52d87-17ec-4fe8-a0a9-321baebe2f15","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/opt/gsb-fleet/workspaces/inst-16/B/task048","sessionId":"12b52d87-17ec-4fe8-a0a9-321baebe2f15","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
