{"parentUuid":"8362fa46-0eab-4d63-b17b-bf290f2fa94c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"Now write the test file. \n\nThe test scenario configuration has been finalized: the account is aa, asset is usdc, initial balance is 2000, fee rate is 30bps. The second payment amount is set to 600, with a 18 fee and 618 charged, leaving a final balance of -122.\n\nThe calculated final balance is only -122, which is below the required minimum of 2122. I've adjusted the initial balance to 3000, and will keep the previous payment parameters and fee rules to ensure both fees are refunded with each payment.\n\nI've corrected the earlier miscalculation of the second payment's fee, adjusted the fee rate to 100bps and switched to more user-friendly parameters, and confirmed the test scenario can be fully refunded under this setting.\n\nThe adjusted parameters produce clean values with non-zero fees for both payments. I've organized the corresponding refund and settlement records, and confirmed the balance view for the account is ready.\n\nI've finalized the design for the Go test function runReconcileDuringRefundInterleave for the refund reconciliation scenario, which supports testing two different failure modes during the refund process. I've sorted out all test steps, including preconditions, concurrent refund and query operations, post-result verification and disk persistence check, with compliance to existing code specifications.\n\nI've added a supplementary assertion to verify that a failed refund application does not occupy a refund success sequence number. This logic will be executed after the read-only re-query checks to keep the query immutability verification clean.\n\nI've confirmed the test execution order: run the query immutability verification first, then add the supplementary assertion to check for non-occupied refund sequence numbers. I'll reopen the disk handle before processing the follow-up refund to ensure an accurate state read, and verified existing test logic can effectively prevent early return of old query snapshots.\n\nI'll run the test with -race flag for verification.\n\nAll expected snapshot records for the test have been fully defined, with verification logic for query immutability, read-only file checks and JSON public format compatibility all sorted out. I'll also add an assertion to verify failed refund applications do not occupy success sequence numbers.\n\nI'll use light assertions to verify sequence ordering and query error types, follow the repo's comment naming conventions, and reuse existing test resources for the two test scenarios. I also need to note that gate counts persist calls.\n\nI've confirmed the persist call counting logic works properly, sorted out the full read-only assertion and disk reopening verification procedures, and clarified that follow-up settlement checks only cover failure scenarios while success scenario extra refund checks are optional.\n\nI've finalized the assertion logic for both success and failure scenarios, confirmed the balance calculation logic and corresponding refund record requirements, and will start writing the test file next.\n","type":"thinking"}],"id":"msg_73201bc58b761858f200da5c","model":"auto_model/urm","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":744,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":105272,"output_tokens":7705,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"stop_details":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"type":"assistant","uuid":"a817ded7-fc37-4ca2-a3c9-7023b73b7bf1","timestamp":"2026-10-07T07:00:50.176Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"12b52d87-17ec-4fe8-a0a9-321baebe2f15","userType":"ex